// Safe:\nint mid = low + (high - low) / 2;"}, {"id": "coding_py_stats_math_h_019", "category": "Coding Questions", "group": "Special Python Topics", "topic_id": "coding_py_stats_math", "topic": "Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "q": "[Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations Advanced #19] In 'Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations', consider a scenario where 68 tasks must be scheduled to minimize total completion latency. If each task has processing time p_i and weight w_i, by which exact greedy criterion should tasks be ordered to guarantee the global optimal solution (Smith's rule)?", "o": ["Decreasing order of ratio (w_i / p_i): highest weight density processed first", "Increasing order of total product (w_i * p_i): lowest product first", "Random round-robin interleaved execution across CPU cores", "Increasing order of raw weight w_i regardless of processing duration"], "a": 0, "h": "Consider an adjacent exchange argument between two tasks i and j.", "e": "By Smith's Rule (exchange argument), for any two adjacent tasks i and j, scheduling i before j yields lower total weighted completion time if and only if w_i * p_j >= w_j * p_i, which rearranges to w_i / p_i >= w_j / p_j. Sorting tasks in non-increasing order of weight-to-processing ratio w_i / p_i provably produces the global minimum.", "step": "Step 1: Let schedule S have task i immediately preceding task j.\nStep 2: Swap i and j to obtain S'. All other tasks are unaffected.\nStep 3: Difference in objective = w_j p_i - w_i p_j.\nStep 4: Objective is minimized when w_i / p_i >= w_j / p_j for all adjacent pairs.", "tech": "Greedy exchange argument (Smith's Rule).", "formula": "\\frac{w_i}{p_i} \\ge \\frac{w_j}{p_j} \\implies \\text{Schedule task } i \\text{ before } j", "code": "tasks.sort(key=lambda t: t.weight / t.processing_time, reverse=True)"}, {"id": "coding_py_stats_math_h_020", "category": "Coding Questions", "group": "Special Python Topics", "topic_id": "coding_py_stats_math", "topic": "Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "q": "[Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations Advanced #20] Consider an advanced placement interview problem on 'Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations'. An input stream of length N = 71 undergoes a sequence of dependent transformations where each element is processed using a monotonic stack. Under what precise condition does the stack guarantee O(N) amortized runtime instead of O(N^2)?", "o": ["Each element is pushed onto the stack exactly once and popped at most once across the entire algorithm execution", "The stack must be pre-allocated with random access pointers and sorted in-place before every push", "The stack requires dual-pivot hardware thread synchronisation at every pop operation", "The elements must be strictly positive floating-point values to satisfy monotonicity"], "a": 0, "h": "Think about the total lifetime of elements in the stack data structure.", "e": "In monotonic stack algorithms, even though the inner while-loop can pop multiple items in a single iteration, every item is pushed onto the stack at most once and popped at most once throughout the entire loop. Therefore, the aggregate number of push and pop operations across all N iterations is bounded by 2N, proving an amortized time complexity of O(N).", "step": "Step 1: Number of pushes = exactly N.\nStep 2: Number of pops <= number of pushes = N.\nStep 3: Total stack operations across all iterations <= 2N = 142.\nStep 4: Amortized complexity = O(N) total, O(1) amortized per element.", "tech": "Amortized aggregate accounting method.", "formula": "\\sum_{i=1}^N (\\text{pushes}_i + \\text{pops}_i) \\le 2N = O(N)", "code": "stack = []\nfor x in arr:\n    while stack and stack[-1] > x:\n        stack.pop()\n    stack.append(x)"}, {"id": "coding_py_stats_math_h_021", "category": "Coding Questions", "group": "Special Python Topics", "topic_id": "coding_py_stats_math", "topic": "Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "q": "[Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations Advanced #21] In 'Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations', you are tasked with optimizing memory cache performance for an array matrix of size 74 x 74. When traversing the matrix in C/C++, why does row-major order (`arr[i][j]`) demonstrate significantly higher throughput than column-major order (`arr[j][i]`)?", "o": ["Spatial locality of reference: adjacent row elements reside consecutively in cache lines, minimizing cache misses", "Temporal locality: column-major traversal invalidates CPU branch prediction tables permanently", "Virtual memory: row-major traversal bypasses operating system page fault tables", "Compiler registers: column indices are restricted to 8-bit register allocation in modern x86_64"], "a": 0, "h": "Consider physical memory layout in C: arrays are stored row-by-row in contiguous addresses.", "e": "In C and C++, two-dimensional arrays are stored in contiguous row-major memory. Iterating `arr[i][j]` accesses sequential memory addresses, loading an entire cache line (typically 64 bytes) that satisfies subsequent reads immediately. In contrast, column-major access jumps across rows by stride of N elements, incurring a cache miss on almost every access.", "step": "Step 1: Row-major layout: arr[i][j] address = Base + (i * cols + j) * sizeof(elem).\nStep 2: Stride of 1 element loads consecutive cache lines (Spatial Locality).\nStep 3: Column-major stride = 74 * sizeof(elem), jumping across memory pages and causing TLB misses.\nStep 4: Speedup can exceed 10x-20x for large N.", "tech": "Spatial memory locality and CPU cache architecture.", "formula": "\\text{Address}(i, j) = \\text{Base} + (i \\times N + j) \\times S", "code": "// High cache hit rate (Row-major):\nfor(int i=0; i<N; i++)\n  for(int j=0; j<N; j++)\n    sum += arr[i][j];"}, {"id": "coding_py_stats_math_h_022", "category": "Coding Questions", "group": "Special Python Topics", "topic_id": "coding_py_stats_math", "topic": "Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "q": "[Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations Advanced #22] Analyze the following recursive dynamic programming state relation for 'Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations':\n```python\ndef solve(i, j, memo=None):\n    if memo is None: memo = {}\n    if (i, j) in memo: return memo[(i, j)]\n    if i == 0 or j == 0: return 77\n    memo[(i, j)] = min(solve(i-1, j), solve(i, j-1)) + cost[i][j]\n    return memo[(i, j)]\n```\nWhat is the total number of distinct subproblems and the overall time complexity with memoization for an N x M grid?", "o": ["Total subproblems = O(N * M), Total time = O(N * M)", "Total subproblems = O(2^(N+M)), Total time = O(2^(N+M))", "Total subproblems = O(N + M), Total time = O(N * M)", "Total subproblems = O((N * M)^2), Total time = O(N^3)"], "a": 0, "h": "Count unique pairs of (i, j) within grid dimensions.", "e": "With memoization, each state defined by (i, j) where 0 <= i < N and 0 <= j < M is computed exactly once. There are N * M unique states, and each state performs O(1) work (two recursive lookups and an addition). Hence, total subproblems is O(N * M) and overall time complexity is O(N * M).", "step": "Step 1: State space = { (i, j) | 0 <= i < N, 0 <= j < M }.\nStep 2: Size of state space = |S| = N * M.\nStep 3: Transition cost per state = O(1) arithmetic operations.\nStep 4: Total complexity = |S| * O(1) = O(N * M).", "tech": "Dynamic programming state-space complexity formulation.", "formula": "\\text{Time} = |\\text{States}| \\times \\text{Cost per state} = O(N \\times M) \\times O(1) = O(N M)", "code": "dp[i][j] = min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) + cost[i][j]"}, {"id": "coding_py_stats_math_h_023", "category": "Coding Questions", "group": "Special Python Topics", "topic_id": "coding_py_stats_math", "topic": "Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "q": "[Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations Advanced #23] When evaluating edge-case safety in 'Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations', suppose an algorithm computes the midpoint of two 32-bit signed integers `low` and `high` where `low = 1,500,000,000` and `high = 1,800,000,000`. Why does the standard expression `mid = (low + high) / 2` fail, and what is the standard fix?", "o": ["Integer arithmetic overflow: low + high exceeds 2^31 - 1, producing a negative number; fix is mid = low + (high - low) / 2", "Floating point rounding error: / 2 truncates fractional mantissa; fix is mid = (int)ceil((low + high) / 2.0)", "Register starvation: 32-bit registers cannot divide odd integers; fix is mid = (low + high) >> 2", "Null pointer dereference: high exceeds maximum array index; fix is mid = high / 2"], "a": 0, "h": "Consider the maximum positive value representable in signed 32-bit int: 2,147,483,647.", "e": "In signed 32-bit two's complement arithmetic, the maximum representable integer is 2^31 - 1 = 2,147,483,647. Summing 1.5B + 1.8B = 3.3B exceeds this bound and wraps around to -994,967,296, resulting in a negative index mid and runtime segmentation fault. Using `low + (high - low) / 2` calculates the offset without exceeding `high`, preventing overflow completely.", "step": "Step 1: low = 1,500,000,000; high = 1,800,000,000.\nStep 2: low + high = 3,300,000,000 > 2,147,483,647 (INT_MAX).\nStep 3: Overflow wraps to negative value: mid becomes negative.\nStep 4: Safe formula: high - low = 300,000,000; (high - low)/2 = 150,000,000; low + 150M = 1,650,000,000.", "tech": "Signed integer overflow prevention.", "formula": "\\text{mid} = \\text{low} + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{\\text{high} - \\text{low}}{2} \\right\\rfloor", "code": "// Vulnerable:\nint mid = (low + high) / 2;\n// Safe:\nint mid = low + (high - low) / 2;"}, {"id": "coding_py_stats_math_h_024", "category": "Coding Questions", "group": "Special Python Topics", "topic_id": "coding_py_stats_math", "topic": "Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "q": "[Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations Advanced #24] In 'Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations', consider a scenario where 83 tasks must be scheduled to minimize total completion latency. If each task has processing time p_i and weight w_i, by which exact greedy criterion should tasks be ordered to guarantee the global optimal solution (Smith's rule)?", "o": ["Decreasing order of ratio (w_i / p_i): highest weight density processed first", "Increasing order of total product (w_i * p_i): lowest product first", "Random round-robin interleaved execution across CPU cores", "Increasing order of raw weight w_i regardless of processing duration"], "a": 0, "h": "Consider an adjacent exchange argument between two tasks i and j.", "e": "By Smith's Rule (exchange argument), for any two adjacent tasks i and j, scheduling i before j yields lower total weighted completion time if and only if w_i * p_j >= w_j * p_i, which rearranges to w_i / p_i >= w_j / p_j. Sorting tasks in non-increasing order of weight-to-processing ratio w_i / p_i provably produces the global minimum.", "step": "Step 1: Let schedule S have task i immediately preceding task j.\nStep 2: Swap i and j to obtain S'. All other tasks are unaffected.\nStep 3: Difference in objective = w_j p_i - w_i p_j.\nStep 4: Objective is minimized when w_i / p_i >= w_j / p_j for all adjacent pairs.", "tech": "Greedy exchange argument (Smith's Rule).", "formula": "\\frac{w_i}{p_i} \\ge \\frac{w_j}{p_j} \\implies \\text{Schedule task } i \\text{ before } j", "code": "tasks.sort(key=lambda t: t.weight / t.processing_time, reverse=True)"}, {"id": "coding_py_stats_math_h_025", "category": "Coding Questions", "group": "Special Python Topics", "topic_id": "coding_py_stats_math", "topic": "Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations", "difficulty": "Hard", "q": "[Python Statistical and Mathematical Operations Advanced #25] Consider an advanced 